// The Appeal, ch1: The Red Jacket. Tuesday, October 27 to Monday, November 2, 2026.
// Game 3 of the 2007 World Series (Saturday, October 27, 2007, Coors Field, Boston 10, Colorado 5) checked against Retrosheet's box score, 2026-10-15.
// Coverage per the bible: scenes LR01x3 LR02x3 LR03x2 RC01x2 RC02 RC08; quiz LR01x3 LR02x2 LR03x3 LR04 LR06 LR10x2 RC01x2 RC02 RC05x2 RC08.
// Searched 2026-10-15: no real Colorado business named Platte Valley Refining or the Night Owl Tap. Five answer choices confirmed from LSAC's April 2025 disclosed test (every item lettered A to E).
// All questions, passages and answer choices are original. Not affiliated with or endorsed by LSAC.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 1, part: `Part One: The Argument`, title: `The Red Jacket`, weeks: `October to November 2026`,
  tasks: [`LR01`,`LR02`,`LR03`,`LR04`,`LR06`,`LR10`,`RC01`,`RC02`,`RC05`,`RC08`],
  cast: [
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, 61. Nineteen years into a sentence at Sterling for a 2007 killing he says he didn't do. Teaches chess and tutors men for the GED. Opens every game with the king's pawn.`],
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's younger brother, 29. Diesel mechanic at a bus garage. Hasn't visited their father in ten years.`],
    [`Joel Harlan`,`Dani's supervising attorney at the public defender's Denver trial office, 52.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer, 58, with an office upstairs from a laundromat on Federal Boulevard. Writes everything in pencil. Owns an old greyhound named Dicta.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Tuesday, October 27, the 6:52 bus into downtown was warm and half empty, and Dani Ruiz had the back seat to herself. She had a used LSAT prep book open on her knees. It had cost six dollars online and came with somebody else's coffee ring on the cover and somebody else's pencil marks all through chapter three. She'd erased most of them.`,
    `She was thirty four, a paralegal for eight years at the Denver trial office of the state public defender, and she was registered for the January test, eleven weeks out. She'd counted. She did one question per stop, a rule she'd made up in September and mostly kept. Read the stem. Read the argument. Find the conclusion before anything else. The book said that on every page, in bold, like it didn't trust her.`,
    `Somewhere past the rail yards the next one came up.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Practice question, page 214">A witness saw a person in a red coat leave the Linden Street pharmacy a few minutes before the theft was discovered. Oren Talley was in the pharmacy that afternoon, and Talley owns a red coat. Talley also argued with the pharmacist last spring. So Talley is the person the witness saw, and it follows that Talley committed the theft.<br>The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument...</prop>`,
    `She read it once. A coat, a pharmacy, a man with a grudge. Easy. She uncapped her highlighter and then stopped, because the book's rule was the conclusion first, and this one had two places near the end that both sounded like the end.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: start at the end`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Where it's going`,
        body: [`Dicta's asleep on the rug, which means it's after six and the dryers downstairs have started on the evening loads. Good. Sharpen a pencil. We're going to do the one thing I care about, and we're going to do it every single time.`,
          `Every argument on this test is a claim and some reasons. The claim is the conclusion. The reasons are premises. You find the conclusion first, before you judge anything, because you can't say what's wrong with a road until you know where it goes. Try this one. The dryers on the left are faster. I ran a load in each on Saturday, and the left one was dry in thirty eight minutes and the right one took fifty. So the owner should put the big loads on the left.`,
          `Ask each sentence one question: why should I believe that? If the argument answers you, the sentence is a conclusion. If it just sits there as given, it's a premise. The thirty eight minutes is given. "The left ones are faster" gets answered by the thirty eight minutes, so it's a conclusion, but it isn't the end. It's there to hold up the last sentence. That makes it an intermediate conclusion. The main conclusion is the claim nothing else in the argument is there to support.`,
          `Indicator words help, and they lie. Words like "so," "thus," and "therefore" usually point at a conclusion. Words like "because," "since," and "given that" usually point at a premise. Usually. A conclusion can come first. It can hide in the middle, right after a "but." When an argument opens with what other people think, the author's own conclusion is often the sentence that turns on them.`,
          `Stuck between two claims? Put "therefore" between them, one way and then the other. The order that sounds like reasoning tells you which one is the conclusion. The thirty eight minutes, therefore the left dryers are faster. Yes. The left dryers are faster, therefore thirty eight minutes. No.`],
        terms: [[`Conclusion`,`The claim an argument is trying to get you to accept.`],[`Premise`,`A reason given in support of a claim, offered as true without support of its own.`],[`Intermediate conclusion`,`A claim that's supported by a premise and then supports the main conclusion.`]],
        exam: `Main conclusion stems ask which choice most accurately expresses the conclusion or the main point. The traps are a premise, the intermediate conclusion, and a choice that says more than the author said. Match the author's claim at the author's strength.` },
      { h: `What a sentence is doing`,
        body: [`The test will also point at one sentence and ask what it does. It doesn't care what the sentence says. It cares what job the sentence has. There are only a few jobs. Main conclusion. Premise. Intermediate conclusion, which takes support from one claim and gives it to another. Background, which sets the scene and proves nothing. Or the other side: a view the author is about to knock down, and sometimes the evidence for that view.`,
          `Do it in pencil. Bracket the main conclusion. Draw an arrow from each reason to whatever it holds up. Then find the sentence the question named and look at its arrows. Arrows coming in and going out means intermediate. Only going out means premise. Only coming in means main conclusion. No arrows usually means background, unless it's the view the author is knocking down.`,
          `The trap I see most: the right answer describes the job, and the wrong ones describe the content, accurately, which is why they're tempting. "It reports how fast the dryer was" is true and useless.`],
        terms: [[`Role`,`The job a statement does in an argument: premise, conclusion, intermediate step, background, or opposing view.`],[`Opposing view`,`A position the author presents in order to argue against it.`]],
        exam: `Role questions quote a claim and ask what role it plays. Name the job before you read the choices. Check both directions: what supports this claim, and what does this claim support?` },
      { h: `Finish the sentence`,
        body: [`Sometimes the argument stops and leaves a blank, usually right after "so" or "thus." That blank is a conclusion, and your job is to write the one the premises actually earn. Not the one you'd like. Not the one that sounds brave.`,
          `Finish it yourself, in plain words, before you look at the choices. Then pick the choice that says what you said, at that strength. Watch for the answer that goes one step past the evidence. If the premises show that a description fits a whole group, the blank says the description can't pick out one member of it. It doesn't say who's innocent. Breaking an argument doesn't prove the opposite. If you take one sentence from me this year, take that one.`],
        terms: [[`Fill in the blank`,`A question that asks for the claim that most logically completes an argument, usually its conclusion.`]],
        exam: `The stem asks which choice most logically completes the argument. After "so" or "therefore," the blank is the conclusion. After "because" or "since," it's a premise that has to close the gap. Wrong answers run too strong, bring in something new, or point the other way.` },
      { h: `Once you have it`,
        body: [`Most of this test lives in the space between the reasons and the claim, and you can't see that space until you know where the claim is.`,
          `If the conclusion needs something the premises never said, the missing piece is an assumption. A necessary assumption is one the argument can't survive without. Test it by denying it: if the denial wrecks the argument, it was necessary. We'll spend next chapter in that gap.`,
          `Sometimes the gap has a familiar shape, and that's a flaw. A description that fits a whole group gets treated as if it fit one member. A change that came after something gets credited to it. Say what the argument took for granted, in the argument's own terms.`,
          `And some stimuli have no conclusion at all. They're just facts, and the question asks what those facts most strongly support. Don't add anything. Pick the modest claim the facts make likely, and be suspicious of words like "all," "always," and "proves."`],
        terms: [[`Necessary assumption`,`An unstated claim the conclusion depends on. If it's false, the argument falls apart.`],[`Flaw`,`A mistake in how the premises are used to reach the conclusion.`],[`Most strongly supported`,`The claim a set of facts gives the best reason to believe, without needing to guarantee it.`]],
        exam: `Flaw stems ask why the reasoning is most vulnerable to criticism. Necessary assumption stems ask what the argument depends on or requires. Most strongly supported stems want what the facts back, not what they merely allow.` },
      { h: `The long ones`,
        body: [`Reading Comprehension gives you a passage of four or five hundred words and a set of five to eight questions. Read it like an argument with paragraphs. Who's talking? What do they claim? What is each paragraph for?`,
          `The main idea covers the whole passage, not one paragraph and not more than the passage says. The primary purpose is the main idea as a verb: to argue, to describe, to challenge, to ask. A detail question has its answer in a line you can put your finger on, so go back and find the line. Don't answer from memory. A function question asks why the author put something there, like to support a claim, give an example, or answer an objection.`,
          `And stay inside the page. If the passage doesn't say it, you don't know it, whatever else you know.`,
          `One set in each Reading Comprehension section usually pairs two shorter passages on the same subject, and some of its questions ask how the two relate. The subjects run from law to science to history and the arts, and you don't need to know any of it going in.`],
        terms: [[`Main idea`,`What the passage as a whole says, at the passage's own scope.`],[`Primary purpose`,`What the author is trying to do with the passage, usually best stated as a verb.`],[`Function`,`Why a detail or paragraph is in the passage: the job it does there.`]],
        exam: `The test is four sections of 35 minutes: two scored Logical Reasoning, one scored Reading Comprehension, and one unscored section of either type that you can't pick out, so treat them all as scored. There's a 10 minute break after the second section and no logic games. A wrong answer costs nothing extra, so never leave one blank.` }
    ],
    video: [`sPWTRkzjw1k`,`LSAT Logical Reasoning: Three Ways to Find the Conclusion of an Argument`,`LSAT Demon`,`6:24`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f1s1`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR01`, title:`Page 214`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She read the argument twice. It was the kind of question she liked, because it looked hard and wasn't, and the answer key was in the back of the book where she couldn't peek without making a production of it.`,
        `The stem wanted the flaw. Before the flaw, the conclusion. Two pieces at the end sounded like endings: "Talley is the person the witness saw," and "it follows that Talley committed the theft." She held the highlighter over the page.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Talley owns a red coat, which is the fact the whole thing turns on.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She highlights the coat and goes looking for what's wrong with it. There's nothing wrong with owning a coat. She spends three stops on it and gets the question right by elimination, without knowing why.`,
          why:`Main conclusion. The coat is offered as true and supports something else. Nothing in the argument tries to prove it, so it's a premise. Ask of each sentence, why should I believe that? If the argument doesn't answer, you're holding a premise.`},
        {t:`Talley is the person the witness saw leave the pharmacy that day.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's close. She marks it, then reads the last clause again, "it follows that," and sees she's stopped one step early. The witness part is a stepping stone. The theft is where the argument wants to end up.`,
          why:`That's a conclusion, but not the main one. It's supported by the coat and then used to support the theft, which makes it an intermediate conclusion. The main conclusion is the claim nothing else in the argument is there to hold up.`},
        {t:`Talley committed the theft. The rest is there to get you to that.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She brackets the last clause. Then she asks the book's question of everything else: why should I believe that? The coat is a reason. The argument with the pharmacist is a reason. "Talley is the person the witness saw" is a step on the way. The end is the theft. Now she can go looking for the flaw.`,
          why:`Main conclusion. "It follows that" flags the end. The coat and the grudge are premises, and "Talley is the person the witness saw" is an intermediate step that holds up the theft. Find the end first, and only then go looking for the flaw.`},
        {t:`A person in a red coat left the pharmacy before the theft was found.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She marks the witness. It's the first sentence and it sounds important, and it's only what somebody saw. The argument never questions it. It's ground the rest stands on, not where the rest is going.`,
          why:`That's a premise, the starting fact. Arguments often open with evidence and end with the claim. The main conclusion is the one the evidence is there to prove, which here is the theft.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s2`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC01`, title:`The box`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She found it two stops later and wrote it in the margin. Could be anyone in a red coat. Then she read her own handwriting back, and the pencil stayed where it was, because she knew that argument. Not from a book. From a courtroom in Brighton, with her father at the defense table, and nobody in that room had said what she'd just written.`,
        `The bus climbed onto the viaduct. She looked at the date on her phone, which she'd been not doing since she woke up.`,
        `Tuesday, October 27. Nineteen years ago it had been a Saturday. The Rockies had played the Red Sox at Coors Field, and a man named Travis Hensley had been hit in the Night Owl Tap's parking lot in Commerce City and died before morning. A neighbor across the street had seen a man in a red jacket do it. Dani's father had owned a red jacket. Dani had said so, under oath, at fifteen.`,
        `That night Teo came by for the furnace. That was what he said, the furnace. He was twenty nine, a diesel mechanic at a bus garage, and he didn't visit so much as fix things. He took his headlamp and his toolbag down to the basement of the house they grew up in, the house their mother died in, and Dani followed him down and took the banker's box off the shelf over the washer. The same washer. It still worked, mostly. Neither of them said what day it was.`,
        `Teo looked at the box on her knees, then at the shelf, then at the washer under it. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You could keep that somewhere else,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Anywhere else."</span> He went back into the furnace closet, and she heard him not leave.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Trial transcript, Wednesday, June 4, 2008, direct examination">Q. How old are you?<br>A. Fifteen.<br>Q. Where were you on the night of Saturday, October 27, 2007?<br>A. At a birthday sleepover. In Thornton.<br>Q. When did you come home?<br>A. Sunday morning. About eight.<br>Q. Did you see your father that morning?<br>A. Yes. He was at the kitchen table.<br>Q. Does your father own a red jacket?<br>A. His work jacket. From the refinery.<br>Q. Did he wear it Saturday?<br>A. He wore it to work that day. I saw him leave in it.<br>Q. Where was the jacket when you got home on Sunday?<br>A. In the washer, downstairs. It was wet. The cycle was done.<br>Q. Who usually does the laundry in your house?<br>A. My mom. On Mondays.<br>Q. Had you ever seen your father wash that jacket himself?<br>A. No.<br>Q. Thank you. Nothing further.</prop>`,
        `She read her own answers twice, standing up. Then she made herself do what the book said to do with any passage: say what the whole thing says, in one sentence, before deciding anything about it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It puts him in the red jacket that day, and the jacket in the washer the next morning.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She writes it on a sticky note. That's all she said. Fifteen years old, a page of answers, and not one of them was about a parking lot. Whatever the jury heard about the parking lot, they heard it from somebody else.`,
          why:`Main idea. It covers the whole excerpt at the excerpt's own scope: the jacket worn that day, found washed the next morning, outside the house's routine. Everything on the page builds toward those two facts, and nothing on it goes further.`},
        {t:`Her answers show her father was the man the neighbor saw that night in the lot.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`She writes it down and looks at it and can't find it anywhere in her own answers. She never said anything about a lot. Somebody else put that sentence together. For nineteen years she's been carrying it like she wrote it.`,
          why:`Too strong. Stay inside the passage. The excerpt never mentions the parking lot, the attack, or the neighbor. A main idea that needs facts from outside the page isn't the page's main idea.`},
        {t:`It records where she spent Saturday night and when she got home on Sunday.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`True, and only the first four questions. She reads on to the jacket and the washer and sees she's summarized the warm up. The prosecutor didn't call her to talk about a sleepover.`,
          why:`Accurate, but too narrow. It describes the opening of the excerpt, not the whole. A main idea has to cover the part the passage spends its time on, which here is the jacket.`},
        {t:`It shows the jacket had blood on it when she found it in the washer that morning.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She goes back to find the line about blood. There isn't one. She knows about the blood from the trial, from the lab, from nineteen years of it. She didn't say it. She never looked.`,
          why:`Not in the passage. You may know it from somewhere else, and that's exactly the trap. Answer from the page, not from what you remember.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s3`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC02`, title:`Why her`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Under the transcript there was a note in her mother's handwriting on the back of an electric bill. Dani testifies Wednesday. Bring her a sweater, the courtroom is cold.`,
        `Dani sat down on the basement stairs with it. She'd told the truth. She'd told it to a room full of strangers while her father sat at a table and didn't look at her, and every word had been true. And here was the part she'd never said to anyone, not to her mother, not to Teo, ten feet away with his head in the furnace. Somewhere in nineteen years she'd stopped being sure he didn't do it. At fifteen she'd been sure. Now she wasn't, and she couldn't have said what year it changed.`,
        `At seventeen she'd asked a lawyer for help with it, once, and been told no. She remembered the no better than the lawyer.`,
        `Behind her, Teo's headlamp moved around inside the furnace closet. She looked at the page again and asked what a lawyer would ask. Why that witness? Why put a fifteen year old girl on the stand to talk about the laundry?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`To prove her father struck Travis Hensley while he was wearing that red jacket.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She reads the page for anything about Travis. There's nothing. Her testimony couldn't prove that and didn't try. It was one brick, and she's describing the wall.`,
          why:`Primary purpose. Too strong. The excerpt never touches the attack. The purpose of a passage can't be bigger than what the passage does.`},
        {t:`To establish the time she got home and fix the timeline of that morning.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The time's there, about eight. But nobody's case turned on eight o'clock. It's a detail on the way to the jacket.`,
          why:`A detail isn't a purpose. Primary purpose covers what the whole excerpt is doing, and the time she got home is a small part of it.`},
        {t:`To have his own daughter tell the jury the jacket was washed out of routine.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She writes it out and has to stop. They didn't need her to say the jacket was in the washer. A detective could have said that. They needed her to say her mother did the laundry, on Mondays, and that she'd never once seen him do it. Coming from her, nobody could call it the State's story.`,
          why:`Primary purpose. Look at what the questions build: the jacket worn that day, found washed the next morning, by a man who never did laundry. And look at who's saying it. The purpose is to put that on the record through a witness nobody could call biased against him.`},
        {t:`To show the jury the jacket was washed on the morning after the fight in the bar.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`That's what the answers say. It isn't why her. The washing could have come in through a detective. The prosecutor wanted the Mondays.`,
          why:`Half of it. It states the content but not the purpose of using this witness. The questions about who usually does the laundry are the giveaway: the point was that the washing was out of routine, from someone inside the family.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s4`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC01`, title:`The closing`, q:`Which choice best states the main point of the prosecutor's closing?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Teo packed up around ten. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"It's the sensor, not the igniter. I'll bring one tomorrow after work."</span> He looked at the box one more time on his way to the stairs and didn't mention it, and she didn't mention the date.`,
        `The closing argument was further down, in a binder with a rubber band that broke when she touched it. She'd never read it. Witnesses weren't allowed in the courtroom after they testified, and by the time it was over she hadn't wanted to.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Trial transcript, Monday, June 9, 2008, the prosecution's closing argument">Consuelo Baca never saw a face. She told you that herself, and you can trust a witness who tells you what she didn't see. What she saw was a man in a red jacket strike Travis Hensley at a quarter past eleven and run. So who wore a red jacket that night? The defendant did. His own daughter told you he wore it to work that day. Ronnie Galvan told you the defendant and Travis fought inside the bar less than an hour before. The lab told you Travis Hensley's blood was on the cuff. And the next morning, before any officer knocked, that jacket was in the washer, in a house where he never did the laundry. The defendant told police he never touched anyone. The blood on his cuff says he did. People don't wash what they aren't worried about. Put it together, and the man in the red jacket was Luis Ruiz.</prop>`,
        `She read it once fast and once slow. His own daughter. Then she did it again, the one sentence version, because if she didn't make herself do something with it she was going to sit on the stairs all night.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Consuelo Baca is a careful witness whose account the jury should trust completely.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`That's the first two sentences. The prosecutor spends them on Consuelo so he can spend the rest on the jacket. She's the setup, not the point.`,
          why:`Main idea. That's an opening move, not the whole. A main idea has to cover where the passage goes, and this one goes from the witness to the defendant.`},
        {t:`Though the witness saw no face, the jacket evidence shows the man she saw was Luis.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She writes it and looks at it. The closing admits the hole in its first sentence, no face, and then spends every other sentence filling it with a jacket. It's honest about the hole. That's what makes it work.`,
          why:`Main idea. It covers the whole closing: the concession that Consuelo saw no face, and the claim that the jacket evidence closes that gap. Main idea answers often have this two part shape when a passage concedes something and then argues past it.`},
        {t:`Washing the jacket proves Luis Ruiz knew he'd done something to Travis Hensley.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The closing doesn't say proves. It says people don't wash what they aren't worried about. Worried isn't knew. She's made it stronger than they did, which is a strange thing to do for them.`,
          why:`Too strong, and too narrow. The washer is one piece of the closing, and the closing claims less about it than this does. Match the passage's strength.`},
        {t:`The defendant lied to police when he told them he never touched Travis Hensley.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's in there. The blood on the cuff says he did. But the lie is a stop on the way. The closing's last sentence isn't about lying. It's about who.`,
          why:`A real claim in the passage, but a supporting one. The prosecutor uses the denial to make the blood look worse, which serves the identification. The main idea is the identification.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s5`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC08`, title:`What she didn't see`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`One line kept pulling her back, the second one. You can trust a witness who tells you what she didn't see.`,
        `Dani had sat through enough trials since to know a prosecutor doesn't hand a jury a weakness for free. She'd never met Consuelo Baca. She'd seen her once in 2008, from the end of a hallway, a small woman in a church dress holding a little girl's hand. Whatever that line was doing, it was doing it on purpose.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`To show the jury that Consuelo Baca had picked out Luis Ruiz as the man she saw.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She looks for the place where Consuelo picked anyone out. There isn't one. The closing says so in its first sentence. Whatever this line does, it does it without a face.`,
          why:`Function of a detail. The passage says the opposite: she never saw a face. A function answer can't contradict the text around the line it's explaining.`},
        {t:`To admit a weakness in the State's case: its only eyewitness never saw a face.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It does admit it. But the prosecutor doesn't leave it lying there. He turns the admission into a reason to believe her, and the very next sentence is the red jacket.`,
          why:`Half the job. The line concedes the missing face and then uses the concession to vouch for her. Describe everything a line does where it sits.`},
        {t:`To turn the missing face into a reason to trust her about the red jacket.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She reads it again. It's a good line. It takes the hole in their case and makes it the reason to trust the rest of what Consuelo said, and the rest of what she said was a red jacket. For the first time Dani wonders what else Consuelo told the police that night that nobody asked her about at trial.`,
          why:`Function of a detail. The line concedes the weakness and converts it into credibility, which sets up the jacket in the next sentence. Function questions ask what a line is for, where it sits, not only what it says.`},
        {t:`To answer a defense claim that Consuelo Baca had lied to the police about that night.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She checks the page for the accusation the line is answering. There isn't one. Nobody in the closing says Consuelo lied about anything. The line isn't a reply to anybody.`,
          why:`Not in the passage. An answer that invents an argument the passage never mentions fails, however reasonable it sounds.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s6`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR02`, title:`Boxes and arrows`, q:`What role does the washer play in the closing?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`It was after eleven. She got a legal pad and drew the closing the way the book drew arguments: claims in boxes, arrows from each reason to whatever it held up. "The man in the red jacket was Luis Ruiz" went at the bottom of the page. The jacket, her testimony, the fight, and the blood went above it.`,
        `Then she got to the washer, and she didn't know where its box went. It was one sentence in the closing. At fifteen it had been the only thing anybody asked her about.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It's the conclusion the whole closing builds toward, the thing the jury had to find.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She writes it at the bottom, and the rest of the page won't fit above it. A jacket in a washer doesn't need a jacket in a parking lot. The closing's last line does. She erases it and moves it up.`,
          why:`Role of a statement. The main conclusion is the identification in the last line. The washer is offered as a reason, so it can't be the thing being proved.`},
        {t:`It's one of several premises offered directly to prove he was the man in red.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`She draws an arrow from the washer straight down to her father. It isn't wrong, exactly. But the closing never says the washer shows who. It says people don't wash what they aren't worried about. There's a step in the middle she skipped.`,
          why:`Close. It is a premise, but it doesn't go straight to the conclusion. The closing routes it through a middle claim, that he had something to hide. Role answers have to get the arrows right, not just the kind of claim.`},
        {t:`It backs the middle claim that he had something to hide, which backs the main one.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She draws a new box between the washer and the bottom line and writes in it: he was hiding something. The washer points at that box. That box points at her father. She looks at it a long time. The washer doesn't say anything about a parking lot. It only says something if you already think he was there.`,
          why:`Role of a statement. Ask what the sentence is for. The closing uses the washer to support "he was worried," and uses that to support "the man in red was Luis." A premise feeding an intermediate conclusion that feeds the main one: that's the job.`},
        {t:`It's the evidence that showed whose blood was on the cuff of the jacket that morning.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She draws the arrow to the cuff. Then she reads the closing again. The lab found the blood. The washer didn't find anything. She moves the arrow.`,
          why:`Wrong job. The lab is the premise about the blood. The washer goes to what he was thinking, not to whose blood it was.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s7`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR01`, title:`King's pawn`, q:`What's the main conclusion of her father's account?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, October 28, Teo came back after his shift with the sensor. The first cold night was supposed to be Friday. He'd brought a drawing from his daughter, Lupe, for the fridge. A purple horse. Or a dog. Lupe hadn't decided.`,
        `Seven o'clock, her phone. Wednesdays it always was, and she took it on the basement stairs. First the recording, same lady every week, where the call's from, that it's recorded. Dani could do the voice. Then her father, opening the way he'd opened every game since she was eight and their board came from a garage sale with a salt shaker for a rook. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Mija. King's pawn, two squares. New game."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"You always open with that."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"So you know where I'm going."</span> Same as every time. Then, like it had just occurred to him, though he'd asked it every Wednesday for ten years: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Is your brother there?"</span>`,
        `Teo had been half inside the furnace closet with a headlamp on when she came down at a quarter to seven. He'd looked at the clock on the water heater and backed out on his elbows. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Sensor's in. You'll have heat Friday."</span> Coat off the chair, out the back door, ten minutes early for nothing. His truck needed a muffler. She'd heard it to the corner.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"He just left, Pa."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Okay."</span> He said it like he'd known before he asked. He usually had. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Tell him I asked."</span>`,
        `The last time those two were in a room together was Mom's viewing, 2016. Luis got an hour. Two officers at the back the whole time, everybody pretending they weren't there. At the casket Luis put his hand on Teo's shoulder (Teo was nineteen, already taller) and said something right into his ear. Dani was six feet off, holding her aunt's purse, and got none of it. After that Teo sat out in his truck till it was over. He never drove to Sterling again. She asked him once what Pa said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Nothing."</span> She let it go. She was good at that.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I know what yesterday was,"</span> her father said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I'll say it once more, and then we play. I watched the game. Two beers. The place was full of Boston people in red, and there was pushing at the bar, everybody shoving. I never hit anybody. I left at a quarter to eleven, by myself, and your brother was at your Abuela's all night, thank God. A man got hurt out there. That's a terrible thing, and his family has a right to hate somebody for it. It wasn't me, mija. They needed a man in a red jacket, and I was standing there in one."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That the State needed a man in a red jacket, and he was the one standing there.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"They needed somebody in red,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Exactly,"</span> he says, with something like relief. Later she sees it's his explanation for the verdict. It's a reason. The claim it's there to support is the one he said a sentence before.`,
          why:`Main conclusion. This is support, his account of why he was convicted. It backs the claim he's actually making, which is that he wasn't the man in the lot.`},
        {t:`That he left the bar by himself at a quarter to eleven, before anything happened.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She repeats the time back to him, a quarter to eleven. He says yes, like she's checking his homework. It's a fact he offers, ground under the rest. It isn't what he's asking her to believe.`,
          why:`A premise. The time is offered as given, to support the claim that he wasn't the man in the lot. Ask why should I believe that, and the account doesn't answer. That's how you know it isn't the conclusion.`},
        {t:`That he wasn't the man who hurt Travis Hensley out in that lot. The rest backs that.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"It wasn't you,"</span> she says, so he knows she heard it. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"That's what all of it's for."</span> He's quiet. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's what it's for."</span> She notices he tells it the same way every time, in the same order, and that Teo comes into it only once, to be put at Abuela's.`,
          why:`Main conclusion. "It wasn't me" is the claim the rest serves, and it's about the lot. "I never hit anybody" is about the bar. It's one of his reasons, like the two beers and the time he left. Find the sentence every other sentence is working for.`},
        {t:`That Travis Hensley's family has every right in the world to hate somebody for it.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She says the Hensleys have a right to hate somebody. He says, <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"They do,"</span> and means it. It's the part he gives away for free, before he says what he wants her to believe.`,
          why:`A concession. He grants it and moves on. Concessions sit near conclusions and sound important, but the argument doesn't try to prove them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s8`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR02`, title:`Nothing`, q:`What job did the washer do in the State's case?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She told him she'd been reading the transcript.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Why would you read that,"</span> he said. Not a question. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"The washer. They made such a thing out of the washer. It was nothing, Dani. I spilled beer on it, your mother was at work, I put it in. A washing machine. They made a mountain out of a washing machine."</span>`,
        `She could hear him smiling, the way he did when he wanted her to let something go. She'd let him, every Wednesday for years.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"You're right, Pa. A washer can't tell anybody who was out in that lot that night."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"See,"</span> he says, pleased. A washer can't tell anybody who was in a lot. The State never said it could. It used the washer to make the blood look like a secret, and she's agreed with him about something nobody argued.`,
          why:`Role of a statement. True, and beside the point. The State didn't use the washer to identify anyone. You have to know what job a claim was doing before you can say it failed.`},
        {t:`"It wasn't nothing to them, Pa. It made the blood on your cuff look like hiding."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`A few seconds of hiss on the line. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Your mother would've washed it Monday."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I know. That's why it was a thing."</span> He doesn't argue with her. He asks if it's cold in Commerce City yet, like Commerce City is another country.`,
          why:`Role of a statement. In the State's argument the washer backs "he had something to hide," and that claim is what turns blood from a bar fight into blood from a killing. Once you can name the job, you can answer him without agreeing and without a fight.`},
        {t:`"It mattered that I'm the one who told them about it. They needed me to say it."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That wasn't your fault, mija,"</span> he says, after a while. She hadn't said it was. She'd said why her testimony counted, which isn't the same as what the washer did in their case, and he takes the chance to drop it.`,
          why:`That's true about the witness. It isn't about the claim. It says why her testimony carried weight and nothing about what the washer was holding up, and a role answer has to name the job.`},
        {t:`"It was the whole case, Pa. Without the washer they wouldn't have had anything on you."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"So there,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Nothing."</span> It wasn't the whole case, though. Take the washer out and the State still had the jacket and the cuff. She overshot, and he heard what he wanted to hear.`,
          why:`Too strong. The washer was one support among several, routed through a middle step. Calling it the conclusion, or the whole case, gets the role wrong.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR03`, title:`Joel`, q:`Which completion follows most strongly from what Joel has said?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`With a minute left on the call she'd given him her move, king's pawn, the answer she always gave. He'd laughed like it surprised him. It never did.`,
        `Wednesday at lunch she'd read her testimony again at her desk, which was a mistake, because Joel Harlan walked by. Thursday on the bus the prep book's reading set was about chess by mail, players who sent each other one move per letter for years. She thought about her father all the way downtown and got two questions wrong. Then her phone buzzed.`,
        `<prop type="text" from="Joel Harlan" time="7:12 AM">Saw what you were reading at lunch yesterday. Come by before you do anything else with it. You're not in trouble. J</prop>`,
        `Joel was fifty two, the supervising attorney on her floor, and the man who'd written her a law school letter two years before she decided to apply. He closed his door, which he never did. <span class="said" data-who="Joel">"Here's how it goes,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Joel">"This office handled his appeal. If you find something, the first question anybody asks is whether his lawyers missed it, and some of those lawyers were ours. We can't be on both sides of that question. So..."</span>`,
        `He waited. He wanted her to say it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"So I keep it off the clock. Nights and weekends, after everybody's gone home."`, s:0, flag:`usedthesystem`, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Joel shakes his head. <span class="said" data-who="Joel">"The clock isn't the problem. Whose files is the problem."</span> She nods like she's heard him. At 6:40 that night, with the floor empty, she types her father's name into the case system. The appeal file is there, and the trial index. She prints forty pages and puts them in her bag under her lunch. It's her login. That's the problem.`,
          why:`Fill in the blank. The premises say the office can't be on both sides of whether its lawyers missed something. The clock was never the issue. After hours, the office's system is still the office's system, so this doesn't follow from what Joel said.`},
        {t:`"So I shouldn't talk about my father's case with anybody here in the office at all."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Joel">"That's part of it,"</span> Joel says. <span class="said" data-who="Joel">"Talking's the least of it. You can't touch anything in this building for him."</span> She'd finished his sentence with the smallest true thing, and he finishes it for her.`,
          why:`True, and too small. It follows from the premises, but it leaves out what matters most. The best completion says everything the premises earn, and no more.`},
        {t:`"So none of it happens here. Not our files, not our hours, not our people."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:3},
          after:`Joel nods. <span class="said" data-who="Joel">"That's it. All of it, not just the hours."</span> He opens his desk drawer and takes out an envelope with her name on it. <span class="said" data-who="Joel">"Letter's still here. Whenever you want it."</span> She doesn't take it yet. She doesn't say no, either.`,
          why:`Fill in the blank. The premises: the office represented him, and any new claim may say his earlier lawyers missed something. What follows is that the office can't be in it at all, and this completion says exactly that, no more.`},
        {t:`"So this office should take another look at it. We're the ones who handled it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Joel winces. <span class="said" data-who="Joel">"That's the one thing we can't do. If somebody missed something, it might be us. We don't get to grade our own work."</span> She'd turned his argument around, and he turns it back.`,
          why:`Reversed. The premises point away from the office, and this points straight back at it. A completion that contradicts the argument's own reasons can't finish it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR01`, title:`One sentence`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The 2010 opinion had been in the box too, the Court of Appeals saying no in eleven pages. The lawyers' names were on the front. For the defendant, Ana Brennan, Deputy State Public Defender. Dani looked her up. Ana Brennan had gone into private practice in 2014. She had an office on Federal Boulevard, no website, and one phone number.`,
        `She called Friday. A woman answered on the first ring. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Brennan."</span> Dani gave her name, and her father's name, and the year. The pause was long enough that she thought the call had dropped. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Monday,"</span> the woman said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Six o'clock. Bring the transcript. Leave the adjectives at home."</span>`,
        `Monday, November 2, the laundromat on Federal was full of the evening wash, and the stairs up to the office smelled like dryer sheets. At the top: a desk, two chairs, a radiator, banker's boxes to the ceiling, and a gray greyhound asleep on a rug, who opened one eye and closed it again. A woman in her late fifties sat behind the desk sharpening a pencil with a pocketknife into the wastebasket.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's Dicta,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She doesn't get up for anybody. Sit."</span> Dani sat and started, <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"My father was convicted in 2008 of..."</span> and Ana held up the pencil.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Stop. Before you tell me anything about him, tell me what the State concluded. One sentence. Not the verdict. The argument."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That my father killed a man, and then lied about it to everybody for nineteen years."`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana doesn't write it down. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's two things, and one of them's your feelings. The lying isn't their conclusion. It's one of their reasons."</span> She waits. Dani tries again, smaller.`,
          why:`Main conclusion. This bundles the conclusion with one of its premises and adds a claim the State never made. One sentence means one claim: the one every reason was there to support.`},
        {t:`"That the jacket in the washer was the same jacket the man in the parking lot wore."`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Closer,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's a step. They needed the jacket to be the one in the lot so they could say the man in it was him. Where does it end?"</span> Dani says it: the man was her father.`,
          why:`An intermediate conclusion. The State argued it and then used it. The main conclusion is where it ends: the man in the jacket was Luis.`},
        {t:`"That nobody else in that bar could have done it, so it had to be my father."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They never said that,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They didn't have to. That's what they needed, not what they said."</span> She taps the transcript. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What did they say?"</span>`,
          why:`That's an assumption, not a conclusion. The State needed it and never said it out loud. You'll want that sentence later. Not yet.`},
        {t:`"That the man Consuelo Baca saw hit Travis at a quarter past eleven was my father."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana writes it on a yellow pad, word for word, in pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Good. That's the only sentence they had to sell. Murder's the verdict. That's the argument."</span> She points the pencil at Dani. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now I'll listen."</span>`,
          why:`Main conclusion. The verdict was murder, but the argument was about who. The jacket, the blood, the washer, and the daughter were all there to support one claim: the man the witness saw was Luis Ruiz.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR02`, title:`Her line`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana went through the transcript in maybe four minutes, licking her thumb for the pages. Dani's grandmother used to do that. Nobody else she knew. Page four, Ana stopped. Pencil point under one line: He wore it to work that day. I saw him leave in it.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"This. What'd this do for them?"</span>`,
        `Dani's line. She'd known it would be since the bus.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Their conclusion. Once he was in the jacket, the rest didn't really matter."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"If that were the conclusion, they'd have stopped there,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They didn't. They needed the washer and the blood and the bartender. Your line was the ground floor."</span>`,
          why:`Role of a statement. A conclusion is what's being argued for. Her line was offered as true and never argued for, which makes it a premise.`},
        {t:`"A premise. It was the one that tied the blood on the cuff back to my father."`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It's a premise,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Wrong job, though. The lab tied the blood to the cuff. Your line put him in the jacket at all. That's how they matched him to the description."</span>`,
          why:`Right kind of claim, wrong arrow. It's a premise, but it supports the claim that he wore red that night, not the blood evidence. Role answers have to get both parts right.`},
        {t:`"A premise. It put him in red that night, so he'd match what Consuelo saw."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana nods. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And it's true."</span> She says it plainly, the way she'd say the time. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Premises are supposed to be true. You did your part right. What they built on it is a different question."</span> Dani has to look at the dog for a minute.`,
          why:`Role of a statement. Her line was given as true and supported by nothing. It held up the claim that Luis wore red that night, which the State used to match him to Consuelo's description. The premise was accurate. The trouble was the step after it.`},
        {t:`"Nothing. Everybody already knew what he wore to work, so it didn't count."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Somebody had to say it in front of a jury,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's what made it count. Everybody knowing isn't evidence. You saying it was."</span>`,
          why:`A premise the argument relies on isn't nothing just because it's obvious. In court, and on this test, it only counts if it's stated.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR03`, title:`Finish it`, q:`Which ending is properly supported by what she's said so far?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now you,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tell me what's wrong with it. And don't you dare say everything."</span>`,
        `She'd been working on it since Tuesday, on the bus, in the shower, on hold with the gas company. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Consuelo saw a man in a red jacket. Every man on his crew wore that same jacket, and half the city was in Red Sox red that night. So the jacket..."</span>`,
        `She stopped. Ana waited with the pencil up.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"...proves my father isn't the man she saw that night."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana shakes her head. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"No. That's the sentence you came up my stairs to say. It isn't true yet. When their argument breaks, all you get is that they didn't prove it."</span> Dani tries again with less in it, and that's the version that goes on the pad.`,
          why:`Fill in the blank. Too strong. A description that fits lots of men can't tell you he isn't one of them. Breaking an argument doesn't prove the opposite.`},
        {t:`"...can't tell you which of those men in red she saw."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Ana writes it under the first sentence. Reads both out loud, flat, like a grocery list. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the case. Not the washer. Not the blood. That."</span>`,
          why:`Fill in the blank. The premises say the description fits many men, and what they earn is that it can't single out one of them. Notice what it doesn't say. Nothing about innocence. Only that the jacket can't hold what the State hung on it.`},
        {t:`"...was never really evidence of anything at all."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Sure it's evidence. Evidence he's one of a lot of guys in red. A jury gets to use that. Just not to get all the way to him."</span> Dani cuts the sentence down. Ana copies it out.`,
          why:`Overstated. A red jacket still rules out everybody who wasn't wearing one. The premises show it can't pick out one man, and that's not the same as showing nothing.`},
        {t:`"...means the police should've looked at Boston fans."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Maybe they should have. Different argument. Not ours."</span> She taps the pad with the eraser end. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What does the jacket show?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Not which one,"</span> Dani says, and that goes on the pad.`,
          why:`New claim. These premises are about what the jacket can show. Where the police should have looked is a separate argument, so it can't fill this blank.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f1s10`, title:`Find the conclusion`, intro:`Ana keeps a stack of index cards in her desk, each with one argument in pencil. Pick the main conclusion on each card.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. "The new bus schedule will cost north side riders time. It adds a transfer at Union Station to every trip from the north side, and transfers there take ten minutes on average."`, choices:[`The new schedule will cost north side riders time.`,`Every trip from the north side now has a transfer.`,`Transfers at Union Station take ten minutes on average.`,`Riders should complain about the new schedule.`], answer:0, why:`The transfer and the ten minutes are the reasons. The cost in time is what they prove. The complaint is never argued.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. "Dicta isn't a fast dog anymore. She's eleven, and greyhounds slow down after nine. Still, she should go for her walk, since a slow walk does an old dog more good than none."`, choices:[`Dicta isn't a fast dog anymore.`,`Greyhounds slow down after nine.`,`Dicta should go for her walk.`,`A slow walk does an old dog more good than none.`], answer:2, why:`"Isn't fast anymore" is backed by her age, but it doesn't hold up the walk. It's a concession, and "Still" turns against it. The walk is where the argument ends.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. "Some people say the laundromat downstairs should raise its prices. They're wrong. Most of its customers walk there from the apartments on the block, and most of them would switch to the cheaper place six blocks away."`, choices:[`The laundromat should raise its prices.`,`The laundromat shouldn't raise its prices.`,`Most customers walk there from the apartments.`,`There's a cheaper laundromat six blocks away.`], answer:1, why:`"They're wrong" is the conclusion, and it means the laundromat shouldn't raise prices. The first choice is the view the author rejects.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. "The streetlight on that corner turns every color orange. So the witness's description of the car's color deserves little weight, which is why the color shouldn't go out in the bulletin."`, choices:[`The streetlight turns every color orange.`,`The witness's color deserves little weight.`,`The color shouldn't go out in the bulletin.`,`The witness isn't telling the truth.`], answer:2, why:`"Deserves little weight" is supported by the streetlight and then supports the bulletin decision, so it's intermediate. The bulletin is where it ends. Nobody said the witness lied.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. "When the clock is nearly out, fill in every blank, even with a guess. Every question counts the same, and a wrong answer costs nothing more than a blank."`, choices:[`Fill in every blank, even with a guess.`,`Every question counts the same.`,`A wrong answer costs nothing more than a blank.`,`The last questions are the hardest ones.`], answer:0, why:`The conclusion comes first here. The other two sentences answer "why should I?" The last choice is never said.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. "It's often said that a confident witness is an accurate one. Research on memory doesn't bear that out: a witness's confidence tends to grow after an identification, while accuracy doesn't. Juries should be told as much."`, choices:[`A confident witness is an accurate one.`,`Research doesn't show confident witnesses are more accurate.`,`Confidence tends to grow after an identification.`,`Juries should be told confidence isn't accuracy.`], answer:3, why:`The first choice is the view the author rejects. The research claim is intermediate: supported by the growth in confidence, and used to support what juries should hear.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Cross examination of Dani Ruiz, June 4, 2008`,
      text:[`MR. ABRAMS: Dani, I just have a few questions. Are you okay to keep going?`,
        `THE WITNESS: Yes.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: The red jacket you talked about. Did your dad buy it, or did it come from work?`,
        `THE WITNESS: From work. It says Platte Valley on the back.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: Does anybody else at the refinery have one like it?`,
        `THE WITNESS: Everybody on his crew. They all have to wear them.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: When your dad left for work Saturday morning, did he say anything about his plans for the night?`,
        `THE WITNESS: He said he might watch the game after his shift.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: And you were in Thornton all Saturday night.`,
        `THE WITNESS: Yes.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: So you don't know what time your dad got home.`,
        `THE WITNESS: No.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: You didn't see him put the jacket in the washer.`,
        `THE WITNESS: No. It was already in there when I got home.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: Did you see any blood on it?`,
        `THE WITNESS: I didn't look.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: Did your dad say anything to you about the jacket that morning?`,
        `THE WITNESS: No. He said, go to bed, mija, you look tired.`,
        `MR. ABRAMS: Thank you, Dani. That's all I have.`,
        `THE COURT: Any redirect? Hearing none, the witness may step down.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: chess by mail`,
      text:[`For most of its history, chess has been a game against the clock. Tournament players race their timers, and the fastest popular formats give each side only a few minutes for a whole game. Correspondence chess runs the other way. Players send their moves by letter, by postcard, or now through a server, and a single game can last a year or more. The form goes back at least to the 1820s, when chess clubs in two British cities played a series of games by post that took several years to finish, and in the twentieth century it grew national and international championships of its own.`,
        `The usual explanation of its appeal is that time improves the play. Freed from the clock, a correspondence player can study a position for days, check books, and avoid the oversights that decide so many games across a board. Some players kept card files of their analysis that ran to thousands of entries, and a few strong correspondence players shaped how certain openings came to be played. On this view, correspondence chess was a kind of laboratory, and its purpose was better moves.`,
        `That explanation has trouble with the present. Most correspondence organizations now allow players to consult chess engines, programs that calculate far beyond any human player, and at the highest level a very large share of games end in draws. Rules against engines would be hard to enforce anyway, since nobody can see what is open on a player's screen at home. Strong players complain that a game can turn less on judgment than on whose computer ran longer. If the point of the form were the quality of the moves, the engines should have finished it, since there is little left for a person to add. Yet correspondence chess has not disappeared. Its players still sign up for events that will take years to complete, and many of them say plainly that they don't expect to win.`,
        `A better explanation is that the attraction was never only the moves. The surviving records of correspondence games are full of what the moves came wrapped in: remarks about the weather and the harvest, apologies for a slow reply, a new baby, and, more than once, a death reported on the back of a card by the player's widow. Opponents who never met kept playing each other for decades, and some learned of each other's marriages, illnesses, and retirements only from the margins of a game. The game set a pace at which two strangers could stay in touch without needing a reason, because the next move was always the reason. Seen this way, the engines changed the chess and left the correspondence alone, and the correspondence was what most of these players were keeping up all along.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Several council members want a traffic light at Elm and Third, where drivers routinely roll through the stop signs. A light won't fix that. The drivers who roll through are mostly trying to get onto Route 9 before it backs up in the evening, and a light at Elm and Third would only make their trip to Route 9 longer, which gives them more reason to rush.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately expresses the overall conclusion drawn in the argument?`,
      opts:[`Drivers at Elm and Third routinely roll through the stop signs there.`,`A traffic light at Elm and Third won't fix the problem of rolling stops.`,`Most drivers who roll through are trying to get onto Route 9 early.`,`The stop signs at Elm and Third should be kept rather than replaced.`,`A light at Elm and Third would make drivers' trips to Route 9 longer.`],
      a:1, why:`Main conclusion. "A light won't fix that" is the claim the rest supports: the drivers are rushing for Route 9, and a light would only give them more reason to rush. The rolling stops are the problem the council members start from, not the author's claim. The Route 9 drivers and the longer trips are premises. Keeping the stop signs goes past the author, who says only that a light won't fix it.`, task:`LR01`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Researchers have proposed that the dark bands in the stalagmites of Marrow Cave mark unusually wet years. This proposal is unlikely to be correct. The bands appear at nearly regular intervals of about eleven years throughout the record, whereas the region's rainfall records for the past two centuries show wet years arriving at irregular intervals. Moreover, stalagmites in a nearby cave that receives the same rainfall show no dark bands at all.<br><br>The main conclusion of the argument is that`,
      opts:[`the dark bands in the stalagmites mark unusually wet years`,`the dark bands appear at intervals of about eleven years`,`the dark bands are caused by an eleven year cycle unrelated to rain`,`the dark bands probably do not mark unusually wet years`,`wet years in the region have come at irregular intervals`],
      a:3, why:`Main conclusion. The author rejects the researchers' proposal, and everything after that sentence is evidence for the rejection. The wet years claim is the opposing view. The eleven year spacing and the irregular rainfall are premises. The cycle unrelated to rain goes further than the author does: the argument says what the bands probably aren't, not what causes them.`, task:`LR01`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Editor: Our readers tell us in surveys that they want more long investigative articles, yet issues featuring such articles sell no better than our other issues. Some staff members conclude that readers don't really want what they say they want. That conclusion is premature. Most of our sales are at newsstands, where buyers see only the cover, and our covers look the same whatever is inside. Subscribers, who know what each issue contains, renew at higher rates after investigative issues.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately expresses the editor's main conclusion?`,
      opts:[`Readers clearly do want more long investigative articles.`,`Issues with investigative articles sell no better than others.`,`Covers should tell buyers when an issue has investigative work.`,`Subscribers renew at higher rates after investigative issues.`,`It's too soon to conclude readers don't want what they say.`],
      a:4, why:`Main conclusion. The editor's claim is that the staff's conclusion is premature, and the newsstand and subscriber facts support it. Saying readers clearly do want the articles is stronger than "premature." Flat sales is the puzzle the staff started from. The covers idea is never argued. The renewal rates are a premise.`, task:`LR01`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Historian: It is often said that the town's textile mill closed in 1898 because it could not compete with newer mills in the South. But the mill's own ledgers show that its orders were growing in its final year. The mill closed, rather, because the river that powered it was diverted for irrigation in 1897. Within months of the diversion, the mill shut down with its order book full.<br><br>The claim that the mill's orders were growing in its final year plays which one of the following roles in the historian's argument?`,
      opts:[`It is the main conclusion the historian draws about the mill.`,`It is an explanation of the closure that the historian rejects.`,`It is evidence offered against an explanation the historian rejects.`,`It is the only support offered for the historian's own explanation.`,`It is a general claim that the historian later qualifies.`],
      a:2, why:`Role of a statement. The growing orders are aimed at the competition explanation, which the historian rejects. The main conclusion is the river diversion. Competition is the rejected explanation, not the orders claim. The orders aren't the only support for the river explanation, since the timing of the shutdown supports it too. And nothing is qualified later.`, task:`LR02`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Nutritionist: Food labels list sugar per serving, but manufacturers choose their own serving sizes, and many choose sizes far smaller than what people actually eat. So a label's sugar figure tells shoppers little about how much sugar they will consume. For that reason, the proposed rule requiring sugar figures in larger type will do little to reduce sugar consumption.<br><br>The claim that a label's sugar figure tells shoppers little about how much sugar they will consume plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,
      opts:[`It is supported by one claim and in turn supports the conclusion.`,`It is the main conclusion that the nutritionist's argument draws.`,`It is a premise offered to show manufacturers set serving sizes.`,`It is a view the nutritionist presents in order to reject it.`,`It is background that has no bearing on the conclusion drawn.`],
      a:0, why:`Role of a statement. "So" shows it's supported by the serving size facts, and "for that reason" shows it supports the claim about the larger type rule. That's an intermediate conclusion. The main conclusion is that the larger type rule will do little to reduce sugar consumption. The arrow runs from serving sizes to this claim, not back. The nutritionist endorses it, and it bears directly on the conclusion.`, task:`LR02`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Beekeepers in the Tamsen Valley have reported fewer hive losses since the county banned pesticide spraying during bloom, and many credit the ban. But the years since the ban have also been the valley's mildest winters on record, and hive losses in the region are driven mostly by winter cold. Moreover, beekeepers in the neighboring county, which has no such ban, report a similar drop in losses. So the drop in hive losses in the Tamsen Valley ______.<br><br>Which one of the following most logically completes the argument?`,
      opts:[`shows that the spraying ban has had no effect on bees there`,`was caused entirely by the valley's unusually mild winters`,`will reverse itself as soon as a harsh winter comes along`,`isn't by itself good evidence that the spraying ban worked`,`shows that the neighboring county should adopt a ban too`],
      a:3, why:`Fill in the blank. The premises give another cause that fits the drop, and a county without a ban that saw the same drop. That earns one thing: the drop doesn't show the ban worked. Saying the ban had no effect, or that winters caused all of it, is too strong, since an alternative cause doesn't rule the ban out. A prediction about harsh winters isn't argued. And the neighboring county's drop came without a ban, which points away from adopting one.`, task:`LR03`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`A good reference letter tells its reader something the reader could not learn from the applicant's own materials. A letter that only restates an applicant's grades and job titles tells the reader nothing that isn't already in those materials. Therefore, a letter that only restates an applicant's grades and job titles ______.<br><br>Which one of the following most logically completes the argument?`,
      opts:[`will hurt the applicant's chances of being admitted`,`should be replaced with a letter from a supervisor`,`was written by someone who barely knows the applicant`,`is less useful to readers than the applicant's essay`,`is not a good reference letter`],
      a:4, why:`Fill in the blank. A good letter must tell the reader something new. This kind of letter tells nothing new. So it isn't a good letter, and that's all the premises earn. Whether it hurts the applicant, what should replace it, who wrote it, and how it compares to an essay are all outside what the premises say.`, task:`LR03`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Opponents of the museum's plan to stay open until ten every weeknight point out that its occasional late evenings now draw few visitors. But the museum stays open late only twice a month, on dates it announces only on its website, and on all other nights it closes at six. Visitors can hardly be expected to use hours they don't know about. Thus the low attendance on the current late evenings ______.<br><br>Which one of the following most logically completes the argument?`,
      opts:[`says little about how many would come to regular late hours`,`proves many visitors would come if it stayed open until ten`,`shows the museum should end its late evenings altogether`,`is due mainly to the work schedules of the museum's visitors`,`will continue no matter what hours the museum decides to keep`],
      a:0, why:`Fill in the blank. The premises say the current late nights are rare and hard to learn about, so their low turnout is weak evidence about regular hours. Proving visitors would come is too strong. Ending the late evenings points the wrong way. Work schedules bring in a cause the argument never mentions. And "no matter what" contradicts the argument's whole point.`, task:`LR03`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Since a regional orchestra began offering free tickets to students last year, attendance at its concerts by people under twenty five has tripled. The orchestra's board concludes that the free ticket program will create a new generation of paying subscribers.<br><br>The board's conclusion depends on assuming which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`Most of the students using free tickets had never attended a concert.`,`The program costs the orchestra less than it brings in from sales.`,`Some young people who attend free will later pay to attend concerts.`,`Every student who attends on a free ticket will become a subscriber.`,`Older subscribers do not object to students getting free tickets.`],
      a:2, why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: if none of the free attendees will ever pay, the program can't produce paying subscribers, and the conclusion collapses. Whether they'd been to concerts before doesn't matter. The board claims new subscribers, not a profit. "Every student" is far more than the argument needs. And older subscribers' feelings never come into it.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`In the five years since its current principal arrived, Ridgeview High School's graduation rate has risen from 78 percent to 88 percent. The principal's policies, therefore, are responsible for the rise, and the district should require its other high schools to adopt them.<br><br>The reasoning in the argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that the argument`,
      opts:[`relies on a graduation rate from only one school year`,`assumes a rise that followed the arrival was caused by it`,`fails to say how the district measured graduation rates`,`treats a ten point increase as a ten percent increase`,`presumes 88 percent is the highest rate a school can reach`],
      a:1, why:`Flaw. The argument moves from "after the principal arrived" to "because of the principal," without ruling out other causes like new funding or a changing student body. The figures cover five years, not one. Measurement details aren't the problem; the reasoning would be just as weak with them. The argument never mentions percent change. And it never claims 88 percent is a ceiling.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`In a study of a coastal salt marsh, plots from which crabs had been fenced out lost their marsh grass faster than plots left open to crabs. The crabs in this marsh eat snails, and the snails graze on the marsh grass. In the plots open to crabs, snails were scarce.<br><br>The information above most strongly supports which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`Snails are the only animals in the marsh that damage the grass.`,`Fencing crabs out of any marsh will destroy its grass in time.`,`The crabs in the marsh eat less grass than the snails there do.`,`Marshes without crabs will lose all of their grass to snails.`,`By eating snails, the crabs likely help the marsh grass survive.`],
      a:4, why:`Most strongly supported. Fewer crabs meant more grass lost, crabs eat snails, snails eat grass, and snails were scarce where crabs could get in. That makes it likely the crabs protect the grass. "The only animals" is too strong. "Any marsh" and "all of their grass" reach past one study of one marsh. Nothing says crabs eat grass at all.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`After the city added protected bike lanes on Grove Avenue, bike share trips that started or ended on Grove rose sharply, while bike share trips elsewhere in the city held steady. Most of the new trips on Grove were made during morning and evening commuting hours.<br><br>Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,
      opts:[`Protected lanes on every street would double bike share trips.`,`Most bike share riders in the city commute to work by bike.`,`Before the lanes, few people rode bicycles anywhere on Grove.`,`The new lanes probably drew new commuting trips to Grove.`,`Drivers on Grove Avenue now take longer to get to work.`],
      a:3, why:`Most strongly supported. Trips rose on Grove and nowhere else, after the lanes, mostly at commuting hours. That supports the modest claim that the lanes likely drew commuting trips. "Every street" and "double" go far past one avenue. The facts are about new trips, not most riders. Bike share trips say nothing about every bicycle on Grove. Drivers aren't mentioned.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately describes what the cross examination as a whole brings out?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`The witness's father came home before the attack in the lot.`,`The witness was unwilling to help with her father's defense.`,`What she knew firsthand was limited, and the jacket was common.`,`The jacket in the washer had no blood on it when she found it.`,`Refinery jackets like her father's were rare in the area.`],
      a:2, why:`Main idea. The questions show the jacket was issued to a whole crew and that she didn't see the night, the washing, or any blood. The passage never says when her father got home. She answers every question, so nothing shows unwillingness. "I didn't look" isn't "there was no blood." And the jacket was common to his crew, the opposite of rare.`, task:`RC01`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`According to the witness, which one of the following had the same red jacket as her father?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`everybody on his crew at the refinery`,`every worker employed by the refinery`,`many baseball fans at the game that weekend`,`her mother, who also worked at the refinery`,`the bartender at the bar where he watched`],
      a:0, why:`Specific detail. Asked whether anybody else at the refinery had one, she answers, "Everybody on his crew." The whole refinery is broader than what she said. Fans, her mother, and a bartender don't appear in her answers. Go back to the line, and answer from it.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Chess engines have ruined correspondence chess for the players who still take part.`,`Correspondence chess outlasted engines because players valued the exchange itself.`,`Correspondence chess served mainly as a laboratory for developing opening theory.`,`Correspondence games last far longer than games played across a board with a clock.`,`Correspondence organizations should bar players from consulting chess engines.`],
      a:1, why:`Main idea. The author rejects the "better moves" explanation and argues that much of the attraction was the correspondence between players, which is why the form survived the engines. "Ruined" runs against the passage, which says players still sign up for years long events. The laboratory view is the one the author challenges. The length of games is a detail from the first paragraph. And the passage never recommends barring engines; it says such rules would be hard to enforce.`, task:`RC01`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`The primary purpose of the passage is to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`trace the history of correspondence chess from the 1820s to now`,`argue that chess engines should be kept out of correspondence play`,`compare correspondence chess with fast games against a clock`,`describe how chess engines changed the way openings are played`,`challenge a usual explanation of a practice's appeal and offer another`],
      a:4, why:`Primary purpose. The second paragraph states the usual explanation, the third shows its trouble, and the fourth offers "a better explanation." The history is background in the first paragraph, not the point. Nothing argues for keeping engines out. The clock comparison is only the opening contrast. And openings come up only as part of the view the author doubts.`, task:`RC02`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`According to the passage, which one of the following is true of correspondence chess at the highest level today?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Players there are barred from consulting chess engines.`,`Most of its games are finished within a few months.`,`Its leading players have mostly stopped entering events.`,`A very large share of its games end in draws.`,`Its games are usually decided by opening knowledge.`],
      a:3, why:`Specific detail. The third paragraph says that at the highest level "a very large share of games end in draws." It also says most organizations allow engines, so they aren't barred. Games can last a year or more. Players still sign up for events. And the passage says games now turn on whose computer ran longer, not on opening knowledge.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`The author mentions a death reported on the back of a card by the player's widow primarily in order to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`show that many correspondence players were elderly`,`illustrate that the cards carried more than moves`,`suggest that many correspondence games went unfinished`,`explain why the surviving game records are incomplete`,`emphasize how slowly mail traveled between players`],
      a:1, why:`Function of a detail. It's one item in a list of "what the moves came wrapped in," given as evidence for the author's explanation that the correspondence was the point. The passage says nothing about players' ages, unfinished games as a pattern, gaps in the records, or the speed of the mail.`, task:`RC08`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Dark on Federal by then. A dryer buzzed downstairs and somebody laughed at it. Ana leaned back, chair creaking like it had opinions, and looked at Dani over the pad. Didn't say anything. Kept not saying it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Do you think he did it?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I think the argument's broken."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That isn't what I asked."</span>`,
    `Dani didn't answer that. On the rug Dicta stood up, turned one circle, and lay down again in the same spot, facing the other way.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"All right,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That'll do for a first night. People climb those stairs and tell me their father's innocent, and mostly I can't help them, because what they want is for me to agree. I don't agree with anybody. I read."</span>`,
    ((s.flags.usedthesystem||G.usedthesystem)
      ?`Ana asked where the papers came from. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"A box in my basement,"</span> Dani said, which was true of the transcript. She didn't mention the forty pages from the case system in her bag, under her lunch.`
      :`Ana asked where the papers came from. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"A box in my basement. My mother kept everything."</span> Ana nodded like that was the best answer she'd heard all week. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Mothers usually do."</span>`),
    (s.score>=27
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You found the conclusion before you went looking for the flaw,"</span> Ana said, to the pad more than to Dani. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Most lawyers can't do that. Most lawyers don't try."</span>`
      :`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You're fast,"</span> Ana said, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"and you're fast in the wrong order. Conclusion first. Every time. We'll fix that."</span>`),
    `No money. She said that first, like it was a condition. What she wanted was Tuesday and Thursday nights, every other Saturday, and Dani wrong out loud whenever Dani was wrong. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And you keep your test date. I won't be the reason you don't go to law school."</span> Top drawer, then.`,
    `A stapled copy of something came out, folded and unfolded so often it had gone soft as a dish towel. Dani knew the first page. She had one just like it in the box. The 2010 opinion. Hers she'd read twice, both times on the basement stairs. Ana's had pencil all down the margins and a coffee ring on the back.`,
    `Ana put it on top of the transcript. Squared the corners. Left her hand on it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Don't tell me he's innocent,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tell me what they had to believe."</span>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch01.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`What They Had to Believe`
});
